<Layout title='Insights — Jennie Muso' description='Insights on website strategy, experience and building by Jennie Muso.' current='blog' styles={['fonts.css','styles.css','editorial.css','projects.css','reference-layout.css','refinements.css','final-touches.css','insights.css','iteration.css']}>
<section class="blog-intro"><p>Notes from the studio</p><h1>INSIGHTS<span>.</span></h1><p>Ideas about design, clarity, and building for real people.</p></section><div class="blog-articles">
<article class="blog-article" id="right-questions"><div class="blog-article-image"><img src="/assets/service-ux.jpg" alt="Interface sketches on a desk" loading="lazy"></div><div><span>Strategy / UX</span><h2>Why a good website starts with better questions</h2><p>It is tempting to begin a website with a visual reference. The more useful place to begin is with the person who will use it: what brought them here, what do they need to understand, and what would help them feel ready to act?</p><p>Those answers shape the structure before the styling. They help decide what belongs on the first screen, which details deserve space, and where a clear invitation matters more than another effect. A distinctive site can still be direct. In fact, the strongest visual choices usually have a job to do.</p></div></article>
<article class="blog-article" id="designing-trust"><div class="blog-article-image"><img src="/assets/bayline.jpg" alt="Warm light across contemporary architecture" loading="lazy"></div><div><span>Experience / Trust</span><h2>Designing trust before the first hello</h2><p>People form an impression long before they send an enquiry. The language, pace, navigation, and small interactions of a site tell them whether a business understands what they need.</p><p>Trust is built through specific, useful information. Show what you do plainly. Make it easy to find the next step. Give important content room to breathe. When every piece works together, the website feels considered rather than merely polished.</p></div></article>
<article class="blog-article" id="right-build"><div class="blog-article-image"><img src="/assets/service-code.jpg" alt="Working at a code editor" loading="lazy"></div><div><span>Making / Tools</span><h2>Choosing the right way to build</h2><p>A visual builder can be an excellent fit for a focused site that needs to launch and remain easy to edit. A custom build can make more sense when the experience requires particular interactions, integrations, or room to evolve.</p><p>The decision should follow the project. I look at who will maintain it, what must change over time, and how the site needs to perform. Whether the answer is no-code, Astro, React, or a mixture, the goal is the same: a strong idea made useful in the real world.</p></div></article>
</div>
</Layout>
